4)Merging Sorted Inventories of a Warehouse
===================================================
A logistics company maintains two sorted inventories of products stored in different warehouse locations. These inventories contain unique product IDs sorted in non-decreasing order.

The company wants to merge these inventories into a single sorted inventory list without using extra space. To accommodate this merge, the first inventory list (inventory1) has extra space at the end to store all elements from the second inventory (inventory2).

Your task is to merge the two sorted lists in-place while maintaining the correct order.

Input Format

An integer m, the number of actual elements in inventory1.
An integer n, the number of elements in inventory2.
A list of m + n space-separated integers representing inventory1. The first m values are the actual product IDs, and the last n values are 0 placeholders.
A list of n space-separated integers representing inventory2.
Constraints

0 ≤ m, n ≤ 1000
-10^9 ≤ inventory1[i], inventory2[i] ≤ 10^9
Both inventory1 (first m elements) and inventory2 are sorted in non-decreasing order.
The last n elements of inventory1 are placeholders (0s) and should be replaced during merging.
Output Format

A single line containing the merged inventory list as m + n space-separated integers in sorted order.

Sample Input 0

3 3
1 2 3 0 0 0
2 5 6
Sample Output 0

1 2 2 3 5 6
Explanation 0

The two inventories to merge are:
inventory1: [1,2,3] and inventory2: [2,5,6].
After merging, the sorted list is: [1,2,2,3,5,6].
Sample Input 1

1 0
1
Sample Output 1

1
Explanation 1

Since inventory2 is empty, no merging is needed. The output remains [1].
===================================================
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String args[]) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int m = sc.nextInt();
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        int a[] = new int[m + n];

        for (int i = 0; i < m + n; i++) {
            a[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        int b[] = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            b[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        int i = m - 1;
        int j = n - 1;
        int k = m + n - 1;

        while (i >= 0 && j >= 0) {
            if (a[i] > b[j]) {
                a[k] = a[i];
                i--;
            }
            else {
                a[k] = b[j];
                j--;
            }
            k--;
        }

        while (j >= 0) {
            a[k] = b[j];
            j--;
            k--;
        }

        for (int x = 0; x < m + n; x++) {
            System.out.print(a[x] + " ");
        }
    }
}
